WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.260
1.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.260
Work on new projects

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.430
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: it was disconcerting
to be validated for something that I believed

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:23.790
22 years ago and that I got a lot of other
people to believe 22 years ago.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:28.360
Including Doubleday to the tune of 2 million
dollars and Disney to the tune of a million

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.550
and New Line to the tune of a million plus
and so on.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:37.530
And then it didn’t happen and then suddenly
all these years later it happened and people

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.440
go “You must feel good to be corroborated?”

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.770
And I said “Yes I do.”

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:48.900
But the truth is it taught me the most important
lesson of all which is what I wrote into an

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.130
essay called The Waiting Room [from Ken’s
blog].

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.640
If I had been waiting for THE MEG to happen
or for any movie that I started 20 years ago

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.780
to happen, I’d probably be miserable (if
not suicidal).

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:08.350
But what you do in the waiting room is you
do something else, that’s how you manager

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.350
your time.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:16.950
When you’re waiting for something, that
can be annoying and a burden and what you

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.320
have to do is other things.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.760
So what I did was 50 other things and as a
result 30 movies have happened and hundreds

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.150
of books and a new publishing company and
lots of other things.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:35.902
Yes it’s satisfying to see that the world
endorses what Steve Alten [Author of Meg:

00:01:35.902 --> 00:01:42.650
A Novel of Deep Terror] and I believed in
22 years ago that this was a hugely popular

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.770
subject for a story.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:51.220
And all the way along brave people especially
Belle Avery who brought it home and Lorenzo

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.670
di Bonaventura and other producers, they made
it happen too.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:03.810
But I guess what it shows among other things
is don’t waste time hoping for something

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:04.810
to happen.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.619
Do your work and then put it out in the world
and let the world take care of it, that’s

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:11.619
one thing.

00:02:11.619 --> 00:02:16.269
And then part of it is to trust the work that
happened.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:22.989
When you create this baby (THE MEG in this
case), if it’s a good baby it will survive

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:25.610
and it will show its muscles when the time
comes.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.770
Maybe it’s been in hiding for all these
years but suddenly it comes out and everybody

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:31.870
knows it.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:38.189
That’s great but what it tells the artist
I think is to focus on what’s at hand, what’s

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:44.319
in your workshop right now and do it well
and then don’t worry about things you can’t

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:45.319
control.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:47.930
Focus on what you can control.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:53.209
And I guess that’s my main feeling about
it is that we did a lot of work on THE MEG

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:58.870
at the beginning, we created its shape and
it finally came out and it did great and am

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.090
I surprised?

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:07.709
No, I’m pleased but not surprised because
I always believed it but I’m so glad I didn’t

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:14.430
hang my own personal psychology on it because
if I’d done that I’d have been locked

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.140
up by now.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.719
Like myself, Steve went on to write eight
more books on different subjects too.

00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:30.700
And built another career around his talent
and he’ll continue doing that.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.209
He’s learned that lesson too that.

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:37.129
Was it disappointing that it didn’t come
out back then?

00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:41.209
Well it felt like it at the time but in retrospect,
things are meant to be.

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:48.309
And I always say to writers that I manage
that every project has its own clock and the

00:03:48.309 --> 00:03:51.989
only problem is you can’t see the clock.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:59.150
So what you do is you put in the work the
best you can and then you screw the screws

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:06.790
on the cover and send it out into the world
and wish it well and turn to your next project

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:12.689
which hopefully you’ve done before you finish
this one project and that’s what the creator

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:13.689
does.

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:16.260
He keeps working on new projects.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.780
So this world didn’t turn out perfectly.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:25.450
God creates another world, maybe it is better?

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:26.840
2.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.840
What No One Else Can Create?

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:28.840
Marc and Elaine Zicree, Screenwriters: And
so what he was doing was creating something

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.320
that no one else could create.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.340
And he told me that he wrote every day for
10 years before he wrote a single line, a

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.600
single word that was uniquely his.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:43.780
And then one day he sat down and he wrote
the words THE LAKE and he wrote a short story

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.790
based on the time when he was a little boy.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.570
When he was eight years old he and a little
girl friend who was seven went swimming in

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.340
Lake Michigan and he came out and she never
did (she drowned).

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.280
And it was a story of her coming back as a
ghost and they told me that when he finished

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.070
the story, tears were streaming down his face
and he knew that he’d written something

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.940
that no one else could have written and it
came specifically from his experience and

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.340
his soul.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:12.370
And it took another two years of writing everyday
before he could write something again that

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.370
was uniquely his.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.920
And then he got to where he could do it again
and again and again and he became Ray Bradbury

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.790
because he was determined to do that.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.790
I thought that was a great lesson to say ‘Okay,
what are you creating?

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:30.250
What can you create that isn’t something
of what others are doing, but that is unique

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.630
to you, that no one else could have created,
that you create something fresh in the world

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:40.110
that’s truthful and meaningful and then
you know you’ve done something worth doing.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.450
So that’s been a great inspiration to me.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:43.450
3.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.450
Six Scripts

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.240
Corey Mandell, Screenwriter/Instructor: I
love my agent.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.970
I think he’s the best agent in the world.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.500
I’m repped at WME.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.150
There is another agent at WME who I think
is the second best agent in the world.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.780
Her name is Adriana Alberghetti.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.920
And she said something once they I think is
so important for writers to hear.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.660
She said that some writers get it and some
writers don’t get it.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.260
And the writers who get it are all working
and the writers who don’t get it (most of

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.569
them) aren’t working.

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:17.560
And so as an agent she just wants to work
with writers who get it and this is what it

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.560
is.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.240
A lot of writers think that to write a script
that changed your life to write a script that

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.310
gets you your first job or to get Ridley Scott
to hire you or to get a show whatever it is

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:30.091
you’re aspiring to, you have a career you
are trying to take to the next level, they

00:06:30.091 --> 00:06:35.330
think it’s about having the right script
at the right time at the right place.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.460
That’s not it and that’s a mistake.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.120
That surrenders all control and power to the
Universe.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.350
The reality is your job is to think in six
script cycles and you have to be writing the

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:53.530
right kinds of scripts, pitch perfect authentic
(we talked about that earlier) and assuming

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:55.320
you trained yourself to be able to do that.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:03.400
You need to write six of those every two years
and the reason is you write six of these pitch

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.950
perfect authentic scripts one of them is going
to go out to the marketplace and just fizzle

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:16.211
and you will never know why and then two or
three of them will go out and they will start

00:07:16.211 --> 00:07:19.370
to get some traction but then something will
happen and derail it.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:23.360
And then there will be one or two that is
absolutely going to happen, you have the director

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.090
attached, you have the stars, there’s a
bidding war, there is no way it’s not going

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:30.890
to happen and something at the very last second
is going to derail it and one of the six will

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:37.460
actually go the distance and sell or it will
be that script that gets you the next three

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.530
or four years of getting staffed on shows.

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.712
There will be one of those 6 scripts that
will just catch lightning in a bottle, one

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:43.712
of those 6.

00:07:43.712 --> 00:07:45.140
One of those 6 (you don’t know which one
it will be).

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:50.640
So if you are not working right now you ought
to be writing 3 or 4 of these scripts a year

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.270
because you are thinking in 6-script cycles.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:57.020
So any one script goes out and it does do
what it needs to you do have to feel like

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.020
a victim.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.870
It’s one of 6.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:03.643
This is the key to working, you have two jobs
now.

00:08:03.643 --> 00:08:06.440
Your day job and your weekend night job.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.620
So if your day job is a feature assignment
writer for Ridley Scott or if your day job

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:14.380
is you just got staffed on JANE THE VIRGIN
(one of my students just got staffed.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:17.000
She’s very excited).

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.590
You have to do everything.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.710
You have to excel at that job.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:25.370
But then on nights and weekends you have to
be writing pitch perfect authentic scripts.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.900
And if you are not writing 3 or 4 a year,
then maybe you are writing 2 a year.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:34.430
But you don’t stop writing these scripts.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.380
Because one of these scripts is going to be
the thing that takes your career to the next

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.380
level.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.390
And so the writers that don’t get it is
I’m working, I’m making money, I’ve

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.390
made it.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:49.700
Plus I’m busy, I have a family, I’m writing
and there is no other time and I get that.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.750
But the reality is the writers that get it
even though they are working they are still

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.710
always creating this new material and they
think about it in these 6-script cycles.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:07.620
And so a lot of times agents drop the writers
who aren’t doing that.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.300
And I get it because when I was a working
studio writer I wasn’t continuing to write

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.300
these scripts.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.610
I was just doing my studio assignment work
and I was making good money, I was just too

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:16.990
busy and I didn’t want to do that.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:18.830
My agent kept telling me to do this.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:19.960
I didn’t.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:25.580
And he said at some point your writing career
is going to end badly and I worked non-stop

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:26.970
for 11 years.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:31.080
But I was a working writer but I never became
an A-list writer.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.320
And the only way I could have become an A-list
writer is to keep writing script.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.500
So there was another writer Erik Singer who
wrote a script and started to get all of these

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:44.830
studio assignments and making a lot of money
but he kept writing on his own time pitch-perfect

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.300
authentic scripts year-after-year-after-year.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.990
And eventually one of them hit.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:55.150
It was AMERICAN HUSTLE and so he is now suddenly
a huge writer.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:59.450
I know the guys who wrote THE NICK (same thing),
they are staff writers and they are making

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:01.310
really good money.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:07.830
They were writing stuff that they really believed
in, THE NICK was one of them and that script

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.690
just took them to a whole other level.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:15.839
Alan Ball with AMERICAN BEAUTY and you can
just go on and on and on.

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:20.880
Often agents drop writers who might be really
great writers but they don’t get it.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:26.080
They don’t keep creating this new absolute…so
it’s either they don’t keep creating material

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.490
or they are not creating the right kind of
material (it’s not good enough).

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:40.750
I have a lot of friends who are agents and
if you keep creating the right kind of content

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:47.130
even when you’re working and the content
is amazing and you play well with others,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.330
it’s very unlikely that they are going to
drop you because that’s exactly the kind

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.839
of person they want to represent.

00:10:53.839 --> 00:10:58.730
And in that case if they drop you, it could
be a conflict with their client base.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:01.860
It could be a lot of things that have nothing
to do with you.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:07.550
But if you are not doing all of those things
and an agent drops you have to look in the

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:08.550
mirror.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:09.550
4.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:10.550
Script Comparison

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:16.839
Carole Kirschner, Author/Consultant: I can
go back to Sean Ryan again (the one who was

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:23.230
so promising) and he got a job on a sitcom
as soon as he came to Los Angeles and then

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.860
he didn’t work for 6 years.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:33.860
And what he did is he wrote and I think that
he wrote about 14 spec scripts.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:40.250
And what happened is his scripts were all
good but nothing was getting him traction.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:47.310
And he asked his manager, he said to his manager
send me an example of a script that is getting

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:53.830
somebody work and his manager sent over the
script that had the most buzz at the time

00:11:53.830 --> 00:12:01.250
and Sean compared it page to page and said
he’d have three things on a page that were

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:02.720
really good.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:08.910
But this one (the one that got the buzz) every
single beat was perfect.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:14.250
And when he realized that he had not been
hitting the mark, he started to write and

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:15.250
hit the mark.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:23.000
And in not too long of a time he got hired
on a show and that show led to another show

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.430
and he developed THE SHIELD.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.390
So you just have to keep working.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:30.390
5.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.020
Nothing in Return

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:35.530
Danny Strong, Screenwriter/Producer/Actor:
I wouldn’t really call it early success.

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:38.649
I don’t even view it that way.

00:12:38.649 --> 00:12:42.540
I view it as 6 years of no one wanting my
stuff.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:47.060
It was some random person who was very kind
who was interested in a script.

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:51.520
But it wasn’t as if all of the sudden I
made it and it’s happening now.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:13:00.160
So it really was a 6-7 year journey to selling
my project which was RECOUNT.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:06.640
And why didn’t give up is because I had
something to say and I loved writing these

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:13.490
scripts and they were important to me and
I was working on stories and it just was artistically

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:21.210
extremely fulfilling where my acting career
was very frustrating and I worked.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:25.660
I worked on some really terrific shows that
I’m really proud of but I wasn’t actually

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.330
getting to act very much.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:34.540
So even when you’re working I would do episodes
of GILMORE GIRLS and I would do 4 episodes

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:39.940
a season and I would work on it a day or two
and that would be one of my only jobs for

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:40.940
6 months.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:46.600
So it’s really about 7 days on set over
a 6-month period so what am I doing the rest

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:47.709
of the time?

00:13:47.709 --> 00:13:52.460
I’m auditioning and trying to get new jobs
but that’s an hour a day maybe?

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:58.320
So really it was a creative outlet for me
of taking my creative energy and putting it

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:00.950
into something where I can actually just go
do it.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:05.050
Whereas as an actor, you have to be picked
to go do it and then you rarely get picked.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:13.529
So I just found it fulfilling and I also was
getting rejected left and right, script after

00:14:13.529 --> 00:14:19.350
script, agents, managers, production companies,
everyone didn’t want my work for 6-7 years.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:26.970
And I think I got to a point where I asked
myself “This is not going well.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.620
What do you want from this?

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:29.890
What are you doing?”

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:34.880
And I asked myself Would I…and it’s one
of the key questions in the movie…would

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:38.620
I do this for the rest of my life if I got
nothing in return?

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:40.360
And I thought yes I would.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:45.740
And then I just kept writing and it wasn’t
as if 6 months later…crack the champagne!

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:52.411
It was maybe three years later and I sold
RECOUNT as a pitch to HBO and just kind of

00:14:52.411 --> 00:14:53.411
went on from there.

00:14:53.411 --> 00:15:02.690
But there was a certain peace in knowing that
it wasn’t about selling something or success

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:04.101
(even though I really wanted it).

00:15:04.101 --> 00:15:11.680
It was a kumbuya moment, but there was a peace
in knowing that whatever happens you just

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:12.950
keep writing.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:15.510
So just keep writing and that’s what I did.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:16.510
6.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:17.510
Story or Character?

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:22.920
Peter Desberg, Author/Psychologist: When Jeffrey
[Davis] and I were starting we took a slightly

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:28.130
academic view one day and said what’s your
prediction about the approach they are going

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:29.130
to take?

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:30.640
Are they going to go more for story?

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:33.460
Or are they going to go more for character?

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:35.790
And we found out the answer is neither.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:39.930
They went for conflict.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:44.050
So if you were going to give us a premise
to develop, the very first step we would take

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:48.580
is Okay whatever the premise is where do we
get conflict out of it?

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:49.580
If there are two characters involved, how
can we make them but heads?

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:50.580
So if you were to give us a premise to develop,
the very first step we would take is okay

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:51.580
wherever the premise is, where do we get conflict
out of it?

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.550
If there are two characters involved how can
we make them but heads to do something because

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.769
everything would come out of that.

00:15:55.769 --> 00:15:56.769
7.

00:15:56.769 --> 00:15:58.750
The Name of the Game

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:06.640
Gary Goldstein, Producer: Writers (as one
example), I get query letters every day, blind

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.890
query letters.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:11.459
“Dear So and So…”

00:16:11.459 --> 00:16:14.519
It could be my name, it could be ‘creative
executive,’ it could be my name misspelled,

00:16:14.519 --> 00:16:15.519
it could be anything.

00:16:15.519 --> 00:16:16.910
“Dear So and So…”

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:22.030
Log line, short description, “Can I send
a script?

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.610
I’ll sign a release.”

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:25.610
Signed…John.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.670
That’s what I get repeatedly all day long
in my inbox.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.120
There is not one lick of research.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:36.470
There is not one lick of anything personal,
like why is this the writer (the only writer)

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:37.870
who could have written this story?

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:45.920
Why was it so compelling that they chose this
out of all the stories in one’s imagination?

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.550
Why did they choose to send it to me?

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:52.880
Now I don’t want to receive the agony and
the ecstasy as a letter but something…a

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:59.769
drop, a kernel, something that stands out
and makes me think.

00:16:59.769 --> 00:17:01.210
Makes me react.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:06.280
Makes me wonder about this.

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.029
But they don’t.

00:17:08.029 --> 00:17:12.100
I can promise you not even 1% do what I’m
describing.

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:16.150
So this is what writers learn from other writers
and the actors learning from other actors,

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:17.150
etc.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:25.610
They learn the habitual tried-and-true, day-to-day
protocol which is usually very unsuccessful.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:29.270
The ROI (the return-on-investment) in sending
blind query blind query letters has got to

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:30.710
be horribly frustrating?

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:33.450
If you’re a business, you’d be bankrupt.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:34.910
So why do it?

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:37.179
Because this is what other people are doing.

00:17:37.179 --> 00:17:43.060
You need to associate with more successful
people and you need to think about it differently.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:48.330
If you were a fashion designer and you took
that approach, you focus on your craft, focus

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:52.170
on your craft, focus on your craft and you
sent out letters saying “Here’s my designs.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.450
Aren’t I great!”

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:56.190
You’d never have a brand.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:57.720
You have to think in terms of business.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:03.270
You have to think in terms of ‘What more
can I do that others aren’t doing?’

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.010
As opposed to ‘What they are doing.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:07.900
How do I stand out?’

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:14.920
Separating yourself out from the herd is definitely
the name of the game and anyone who has invested

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:22.340
that blood, sweat and tears, that sort of
soul equity of investing all of that time

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:30.260
writing 120 or 110 pages (whatever) of story
and rewriting it until it is just so.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:32.480
But they want to share their wares with the
world.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:35.050
Now they’re so proud of this.

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:40.830
They should invest a like amount of time figuring
out how to market and how to be a business

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:45.440
person and how to reach out to people and
how to do things differently than all of their

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:46.440
peers.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:47.440
Like that’s a great measurement.

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:51.350
Am I doing what everyone else is doing or
am I being somehow, putting myself out into

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:54.980
the world and speaking of myself to the right
people in ways that matter?

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:55.980
8.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.180
The Great Weakness

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:03.470
John Truby, Author/Screenwriter/Instructor:
A common notion (particularly about action

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:10.870
heroes), notion was always in the past that
when you have an action hero or especially

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:17.429
a super hero that you want a character who
begins as a good person (an upstanding moral

00:19:17.429 --> 00:19:22.860
paragon) and remains that way throughout the
entire story.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:29.429
But about 10 years ago Hollywood discovered
that that’s not the best way to tell the

00:19:29.429 --> 00:19:30.920
super hero story.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:36.640
That’s one of those examples of conventional
wisdom that’s not so.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:43.090
What we care about is to see a character overcome
a deep weakness.

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:50.610
Now the audience thinks the story is all about
the hero achieving his goal because then we’ve

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:56.510
got success and that’s important and in
most stories the hero will accomplish his

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.970
goal by the end of the story.

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:03.150
That is not what the audience is most interested
in.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:11.020
They can’t tell you this but we know from
lots of experience (hundreds of years of storytelling

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:17.770
that the real fact is not does the hero accomplish
the goal but does the hero overcome the great

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:22.560
weakness because that’s what makes us care
about that character.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:23.560
We see them in pain.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:25.299
We see them in trouble, right?

00:20:25.299 --> 00:20:31.460
If they are already successful if they are
already good (and in some cases) a perfect

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:34.440
person, where do they go?

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:39.400
There is nothing for them to overcome.

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:46.860
So that’s why it’s so important to show
in the very opening pages of our script the

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:48.990
internal weakness of our character.

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:56.400
Now we may also want to give them qualities
that are likable qualities that the audience

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.710
can also hook onto.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:03.530
Those are not nearly as important as establish
the weakness up front in the story.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:04.530
9.

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:05.940
I’ll Take A Look At It

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:12.500
Jen Grisanti, Author/Consultant: I’ll never
forget when I was a studio executive.

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:20.040
There was a certain circumstance where this
happened where we had the network, the studio,

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.640
the show runner and the writer on the phone.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:32.370
And the writer was so defensive to the network
notes.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:44.961
And it was my job to go to the writer and
say you need to really hear the note, understand

00:21:44.961 --> 00:21:52.810
the note, and then you pat best response that
isn’t going to put someone on the defense

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:56.410
and isolate someone is “I will take a look
at it.”

00:21:56.410 --> 00:22:06.060
That helps to…first of all it’s positive
on the writer.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:10.490
It’s saying that they are open (it’s not
saying that they agree with the note) but

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:15.870
it’s saying that they are open to take a
look at it and that’s all people want to

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:16.870
know.

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:22.030
What I think the biggest thing writers have
to think about when it comes to receiving

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:30.890
notes on their script is that even if they
don’t agree with the note at face value,

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:35.850
they have to understand there might be a note
under the note.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:42.580
And the note that they need to make to themselves
is something isn’t working and I have to

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:49.190
figure out how to make it work because if
this person who know story is not getting

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:55.040
it, there is something going on in my process
that isn’t working and just be open to that.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.490
I mean the gift with writing is and what I
always say to writers is that you have to

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:09.950
recognize first of all what is the blank page
is the only thing between them and a writing

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:12.760
career.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:18.620
And how they fill that page is what makes
the difference.

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:25.480
So they can rewrite anything, that’s why
I say to writers I cannot tell you (it was

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:33.960
my job) how many show runners I had to talk
off the ledge after they heard notes and after

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:34.960
they heard notes.

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:41.530
After they heard notes from the studio or
the owner of the company, I had to be the

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:52.360
peacemaker and I had to really help them to
see at first okay I think the writers mind

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:53.360
may go “Oh my gosh.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:56.320
This is a total page 1 rewrite.”

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:58.030
Very often it is not.

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:05.740
All it is is really thinking about the note,
understanding the note, and then if they are

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:14.559
open to it what I’ve seen (the best writer
for me) knows how to make the note go beyond

00:24:14.559 --> 00:24:15.559
the note.

00:24:15.559 --> 00:24:16.820
And that takes openness.

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:24.320
That takes advanced ability to really know
‘Alright I’m going to make that note there

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:30.880
therefore that’s going to effect a later
moment so I’m going to change that later

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:37.190
moment so that it links with what I just changed
and they know how to do that.

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:48.600
I think the biggest wall note is saying “You’re
wrong.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.179
I don’t agree.”

00:24:51.179 --> 00:25:04.910
I think that shows lack of openness and accessibility
and desire to make the story better.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:14.750
Or writers will say I don’t get it and then
the executive or the story consultant will

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:17.930
go through it again.

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:25.400
And to the person giving the note there is
definitely a tremendous amount of responsibility.

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:30.240
I am definitely a believer that if you are
going to give a note.

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:31.240
And this is where I think to the person giving
the note there is also tremendous responsibility.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:32.400
If you are going to give a note, give an example.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:33.679
Show them what you mean.

00:25:33.679 --> 00:25:37.120
So that they understand the note.

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:41.510
There are many consultants who will say “I
don’t think that’s my job.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:42.560
I’m not the writer.

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:43.809
They are the writer.”

00:25:43.809 --> 00:25:45.080
I am a believer.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:52.840
I come from the studio executive perspective
where clarity is key in the outcome of the

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:53.840
story.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:59.320
So if you going to give a note and give an
example of why it’s not working or what

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:05.809
you mean by it then you are going to have
a stronger chance of getting the outcome that

00:26:05.809 --> 00:26:06.809
you want.

00:26:06.809 --> 00:26:09.640
Elaine Zicree, Screenwriter: It’s a funny
thing but the thing that most affected me

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:16.050
was a very good script doctor that I had and
it was a script that I’d written.

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:18.100
It was one of my most favorite scripts I’d
ever written and it was one of my favorite

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:19.100
script ultimately.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:21.150
But she said “This is brilliant.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.190
I absolutely love it.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:26.760
Now here’s the notes!”

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:31.341
But the fact that she said that I was brilliant
and that she loved it I thought “Well what

00:26:31.341 --> 00:26:32.341
the heck?

00:26:32.341 --> 00:26:33.490
What’s with a few notes?

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:34.540
I’m brilliant.”

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:42.510
So I think crazy flattery is sort of a way
of allowing somebody to feel special.

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:45.100
With crazy flattery all those notes seemed
like nothing.

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:52.340
You know one of the biggest pieces of advice
was “Hey, what you are doing has super value.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:53.900
Now here is the work.”

00:26:53.900 --> 00:27:00.400
And just going on that thing where I was allowed
to have super value at the top of the journey

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:04.630
really got me through as thought it was nothing
of those notes.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:05.630
10.

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:06.660
How To Build Empathy

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:11.030
Karl Iglesias, Author/Screenwriter/Instructor:
For character (and I talk about this in my

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:16.080
Pixar seminar) because they are masters of
this.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:30.340
They tell stories about toys, about monsters,
about bugs, about robots, a fish, a rat.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:34.700
They make you care and connect with those
characters.

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:39.730
So there are a whole bunch of techniques and
you can see what they do and what the techniques

00:27:39.730 --> 00:27:41.500
are.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:47.250
For me it’s like there are three things
that you do is that you make us feel sorry

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:52.159
for that character, there are little moments
where you can create a moment in the story

00:27:52.159 --> 00:28:03.330
where a character is unjustly abused or unjustly
mistreated or insulted or betrayed or neglected.

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:09.230
And so it can be any character and if that
moment is there you are going to feel sorry

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:10.530
for that character (at that moment).

00:28:10.530 --> 00:28:12.690
It takes an instant.

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:13.730
So that’s one.

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:16.080
If you show that they are like us.

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:17.570
If you show their humanity for example.

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:22.419
If you show that they care about something
other than themselves, that’s another technique.

00:28:22.419 --> 00:28:30.840
So there’s a moment in the movie LEON THE
PROFESSIONAL which is about the bad guy right

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:32.930
and it opens with him doing something bad
and he hurts someone.

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:34.830
You don’t know if he’s good or bad.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:39.400
But he goes home and he takes care of a plant
and right away you say “Oh?

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:40.690
He cares about a plant.

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:42.280
So he’s okay.

00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:43.340
He’s human.”

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:46.750
The other part is admiration.

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:52.350
So if you are dating somebody or if you are
trying to find somebody who is a good match

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:57.070
for you, that list of things that you like
in a person that’s an admirable trait.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:02.309
So if someone is funny, who is responsible,
who is courageous, a whole list of things

00:29:02.309 --> 00:29:06.309
you can add to a character to say “I admire
this.”

00:29:06.309 --> 00:29:09.760
Usually they are like the best at what they
do.

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:18.280
The best ad executive or best agent or the
best cop…courageous…I have a whole bunch

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:19.880
of them.

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:21.299
The list is all in the book.

00:29:21.299 --> 00:29:23.510
There are all these lists of what you can
do.

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:30.700
So if you do all of these things, if you show
clips of the moment you meet the character

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:35.559
(it’s usually like a three minute scene)
you can see all of these things being applied

00:29:35.559 --> 00:29:38.060
in like 3 minutes.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:42.370
The opening of WALLE after he’s done with
the garbage and he comes to his little house,

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:48.179
it’s a 3-minute scene and there’s about
20 of these techniques done.

00:29:48.179 --> 00:29:52.330
This is how you connect with that character
in that one scene emotionally.

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:56.470
I also show a clip of an ad (it’s a commercial)
about a lamp.

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:58.850
It’s a one minute thing.

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:07.690
It’s a great ad for Ikea (award-winning
commercial) where they make you care about

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:08.690
the lamp.

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:12.730
And the joke about it is a guy comes out and
says “You probably had feeling for this

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:14.690
lamp because you’re crazy.

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:17.110
The lamp is only a lamp.

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:18.140
And the new one is better.”

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:20.700
The new one to replace it.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:28.180
But it’s amazing once you know those techniques
and then you see them in action and you go

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:29.180
“Oh my gosh?”

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:33.070
I kind of always feel (I always give this
caveat with my students and to everybody who

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:38.679
hears me speak) that if you love stories (if
you love film) and you don’t want me to

00:30:38.679 --> 00:30:44.539
destroy the illusion do not listen to me because
I feel like I’m one of the magicians who

00:30:44.539 --> 00:30:47.880
gives you the trick of how it’s done and
then you see the trick and it’s not the

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:48.880
same anymore.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:53.080
So I always feel bad about that because once
you know the techniques and you see a film

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:54.170
you’re are going to see them everywhere.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:31:01.100
It’s going to kind of ruin it for you so
just fair warning right there that this is

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:04.030
how we do it and it creates that effect.

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:05.030
11.

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:06.030
A Participatory Experience

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:12.039
Michael Hauge, Author/Consultant: It’s not
an audience’s or a reader’s job to feel

00:31:12.039 --> 00:31:15.039
something you have to elicit.

00:31:15.039 --> 00:31:20.900
So that means you haven’t created sufficient
conflict or emotion in your story to elicit

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:27.100
or you haven’t created a premise (a concept
of a story) or you have it structured in such

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:32.640
a way that the conflict makes any sense or
can be emotionally involving because you’ve

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:37.150
given them nothing to route for or you haven’t
created empathy with the hero or whatever

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:38.150
it might be.

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:43.480
But it’s your job to elicit emotion and
emotion has to come naturally from the reader

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:47.940
and audience because of what you’ve done.

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:52.550
They have to be participating in the story
that you created.

00:31:52.550 --> 00:32:00.610
That’s why for example empathy is so important
for you to create for your hero because a

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:05.940
story is a participatory experience for the
audience and the listener and the reader.

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:08.530
We become that character.

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:11.669
We want to experience the same emotion as
they do.

00:32:11.669 --> 00:32:16.049
We don’t go to the movies because it’s
interesting to watch people do things.

00:32:16.049 --> 00:32:19.029
It’s emotional for us to get to do them.

00:32:19.029 --> 00:32:20.789
We become Jason Bourne.

00:32:20.789 --> 00:32:24.790
We become Rose as she falls in love with Jack
and she’s on the Titanic.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:32.010
We become the astronaut who is out in space
in GRAVITY.

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:33.120
We are a part of it.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:37.169
We are participating in the action, in the
events of that story.

00:32:37.169 --> 00:32:43.380
But we have no basis, no way to get into the
story if on a subconscious level become the

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:48.500
hero of that story and that’s what the empathy
and identification you create at the beginning

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:49.900
is going to do.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:55.070
So if you’ve created a story with an empathetic
hero so we participate, giving them a clear

00:32:55.070 --> 00:33:01.080
goal, and created enough logical and believable
obstacles to overcome then the emotion will

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:06.830
grow organically out of your audience because
they are in it and you’ve created the conflict

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:08.020
that will do that.

00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:14.429
To me all roads lead to the outer motivation
and so if all you are doing is stringing together

00:33:14.429 --> 00:33:19.279
obstacles to overcome and there is a very
similar mistake and that is people who think

00:33:19.279 --> 00:33:23.100
comedy is one funny thing after another happening.

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:27.309
That’s not what a story is.

00:33:27.309 --> 00:33:34.330
Story is a sequence of events experienced
by a hero who is pursuing a very specific

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:35.330
goal.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:39.820
So if you are just throwing one obstacle after
another but we don’t care the hero or the

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:44.140
hero or it’s not clear why the hero is doing
this or what they are trying to achieve or

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:51.160
if those obstacles are illogical or if the
pace of the story lags, every thing is at

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:56.461
a very high level of emotion and creating
sort of peaks and valleys at an emotional

00:33:56.461 --> 00:34:02.130
level so you have big moments and quiet moments
and moments of connection and moments of separation

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:03.130
and so on.

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:08.520
There are all those things that you do in
addition to throwing a bunch of things at…you

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:12.039
know car chases and fight scenes up on the
screen.

00:34:12.039 --> 00:34:15.730
Which many people have tried and those movies
don’t succeed.

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:22.700
If it’s just car chases and fight scenes
it’s just a series of videos really.

00:34:22.700 --> 00:34:26.540
But it’s not a story or a movie where people
are going to be involved in.

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.169
It has to alternate.

00:34:28.169 --> 00:34:33.889
It’s not a resting point as if you stay
there forever but it’s like in a comedy

00:34:33.889 --> 00:34:35.399
you don’t want joke…joke…joke…joke.

00:34:35.399 --> 00:34:41.690
There’s got to be a serious moment or two
and look at any great comedy (look at any

00:34:41.690 --> 00:34:45.470
decent comedy) and there’s going to be something
very funny and then there’s going to be

00:34:45.470 --> 00:34:51.369
something touching or sad or romantic or there
is going to be a deeper connection between

00:34:51.369 --> 00:34:56.000
the characters or there is going to be something
more exciting or there is going to be something

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:57.000
softer.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:02.970
You want to vary the peaks and valleys, the
emotional level and pitch of the story as

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:08.010
we go steadily toward the finish line that
the hero is trying to reach.

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:14.270
So the obstacle is getting bigger, they need
to come closer together so the pace will accelerate

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:20.240
but if there’s only obstacles (one right
after the other) with no chance to catch your

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:25.460
breath, no chance to participate what might
be coming, no chance to explain what’s going

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:30.570
on and no chance to get closer and deeper
into the character, it’s going to be a very,

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:35.790
very shallow story with very limited emotional
involvement or appeal.

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:36.790
12.

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:37.790
Writer’s Mindset

00:35:37.790 --> 00:35:40.510
Erik Oleson, Writer of MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE
and ARROW:

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:47.070
My process evolves every time I do another
job I realize how much I didn’t know in

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:48.890
my prior job.

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:57.020
So I’m somebody who reads pretty much reads
every trick, every screenwriting book, every

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:01.650
script that I can get my hands on and I’m
constantly trying to evolve as a craftsman.

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:07.490
I’m also fortunate enough in this job to
surround myself with really talented people

00:36:07.490 --> 00:36:11.619
and you learn a lot from other writers.

00:36:11.619 --> 00:36:17.549
Like there are writers on MAN IN THE HIGH
CASTLE who have won Emmy’s…like Martin

00:36:17.549 --> 00:36:19.490
Scorsese best friend is another one.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:24.720
You want to really kind of learn from the
people around you and that just makes you

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:25.720
better.

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:28.849
So I can’t say that I have any set process.

00:36:28.849 --> 00:36:33.430
Although right now as I’m developing something
I have to say that I’m a big fan of the

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:37.520
John Truby stuff.

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:41.290
I think he kind of hits the sweet spot for
me.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:48.170
But I’ve taken them all, I’ve taken McKee,
I’m one of these guys who likes to read

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:55.400
new stuff about the craft and I figure if
I can learn something from any book, if I

00:36:55.400 --> 00:37:00.390
get one thing out of it and it makes me a
better writer, I’m all for it.

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:03.910
Christine Conradt, Screenwriter: Just being
able to recognize your deficiencies and for

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:09.290
me when I read a great script and I go “Oh
my gosh this is a great script.”

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:12.720
I get really excited and go “What are they
doing that I’m not doing.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:16.030
What can I learn from what this writer did?”

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:21.950
And so I think just constantly learning and
constantly reading and constantly researching

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:28.000
and having that in the back of your mind all
the time that you want to get better, you

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:33.349
will just gravitate towards things that make
you better.

00:37:33.349 --> 00:37:38.040
Instead of finding that comfortable place
where you know you are good at writing this

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:41.900
so I can write this over and over, it’s
forcing yourself to stretch.

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:47.280
Because I think once you do that it’s a
great feeling that you’ve written something

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:50.810
good and you’ve done something you’ve
never done before, then you have something

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:51.900
you can feel really proud of.

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:55.530
You can learn from everyone and that’s the
thing, there are so many people who…I think

00:37:55.530 --> 00:38:00.130
people just want to feel validated so badly
where they get to a place where they say “I’m

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:03.800
an expert and everyone should just be listening
to what I have to say about this.”

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:11.140
And it’s kind of crazy because yes you may
have put your 10,000 hours in, you may be

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:15.680
good at what you do, but you can learn something
from everything that you read, even learning

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:17.300
what not to do.

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:21.060
Even in really bad scripts you will find these
little moments where you’re like “Oh my

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:25.430
gosh, that was really well-conveyed.”

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:30.980
So just being open to those things and learning
from other people because people are constantly

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:36.340
adding to this body of work that exists in
the world and there is so much for you to

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:38.440
be able to read out there.

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:41.010
Just be open to what other people are doing.

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:44.190
But then again you have to love reading.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:46.080
So many writers don’t love reading.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.480
So read, read, read, read.

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:52.680
A lot of people want to start writing without
having read a script.

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:54.400
I just don’t understand it.

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:59.170
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: Probably the
most important perspective has been to see

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.640
it as something I’m always learning and
I’m always growing and that that’s okay.

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:06.270
It’s not like I’m supposed to know it
all or be perfect at it and the things that

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:10.100
I write are just going to be great automatically
and others are going to love them.

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:17.180
To see it as it’s an ongoing quest of kind
of a self-development, self-education, I’m

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:23.730
learning with everything that I write, what
works better and not as good so it’s like

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:30.630
this journey of development that you are going
to sort of enjoy the journey as opposed to

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:35.580
I have to get to a certain place which it
is easy to feel that way because the world

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:39.510
works that way where I have to get the agent,
I have to get the sale, I have to get something

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:42.080
produced, I have to get the money to make
my movie.

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:45.550
There are these goals who might have in the
world that are a lot of times out of our control

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:51.410
and if you focus only on those goals like
what I can get, what I can achieve instead

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:53.859
of what can I express?

00:39:53.859 --> 00:39:58.160
How can I just do what I’ve decided I want
to do which is be a writer and be learning

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:01.830
and growing and find some satisfaction in
that journey.

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:06.730
Because when you turn it into there have to
be these quantifiable in terms of money or

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:11.260
other’s reactions or whatever, then you
are putting all your power in this thing that

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:16.250
may or may not ever happen, that you can’t
for sure make happen no matter what you do.

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:23.560
So I think it seems counterproductive because
to be successful (be a professional) you want

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:27.050
to focus on the professional goals.

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:33.210
Well you do to some extent and you are open
to feedback and you are listening and educating

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:34.210
yourself.

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:38.990
I didn’t say just go in a hole and do what
you want to do and shut the outside world

00:40:38.990 --> 00:40:39.990
out.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.849
I’m saying engage with the world, with feedback,
with education, with trying to understand

00:40:43.849 --> 00:40:48.430
and get better so it’s growth but making
it a positive growth for your self that is

00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:54.470
about making me better and making my writing
better not making my results better.

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:57.240
Because when you focus on how do I give more.

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.130
How do I write something that is really going
to impact people?

00:41:00.130 --> 00:41:01.130
How do I do that?

00:41:01.130 --> 00:41:02.619
How do I figure out how to do that?

00:41:02.619 --> 00:41:04.230
How do I get better at it?

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:10.530
You are then about giving to other people
and you will achieve success I believe more

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:16.900
when you succeed at giving more, you will
get more automatically as opposed to how do

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:18.260
I get the success?

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:19.520
How do I get the breaks?

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.191
How do I get the right person to read it?

00:41:21.191 --> 00:41:22.720
How do I get people to like it?

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:28.830
It’s more how do I create something so wonderful
that people will just automatically like it

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:30.940
and I’m not even concerning myself with
that.

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:35.910
It’s about taking the power into your own
hands and being about how you can improve

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.920
your stuff and your self as a writer.

00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:44.801
Having that approach I think is a stronger,
healthier approach that is going to lead to

00:41:44.801 --> 00:41:47.760
more success more then if you do it the other
way.

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:48.760
13.

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:49.760
Not Good Enough

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:56.300
Eric Edson, CSUN Screenwriting Professor:
The number one reason why you are not making

00:41:56.300 --> 00:42:05.860
it is the material is not yet good enough
because especially if you are not reading

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:12.530
the stuff that is out there you have no way
of knowing how good good has to be in order

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:14.180
to get noticed.

00:42:14.180 --> 00:42:20.490
Coming in you have to be better than most
of the people who are now making a living

00:42:20.490 --> 00:42:24.330
at it in order to get noticed.

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:33.420
But the contests offer…re-objectification…the
contests offer the concrete way to do that.

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:39.490
Work on it, work on it, until you’ve got
something you’re ready to test.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:43.099
But now pick your contests carefully.

00:42:43.099 --> 00:42:47.860
The first time you try it out pick smaller
contests (more fringy).

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:49.040
I mean do your research.

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:53.900
They should be decent, upstanding contests
and so forth.

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:56.670
Try it out…try it out.

00:42:56.670 --> 00:43:02.320
Did you make the quarter finals or were you
swept away in the first pass.

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:08.930
Okay if that happens, you have learned something,
it’s not ready.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:12.160
And you keep working on it.

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:20.500
And finally when you get to the mid-level
ranks and your script gets to the quarter

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:22.080
finals okay.

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:25.600
It did not make the top ten or the top 15.

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:29.140
Fine…you’ve learned something more.

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:33.750
Go back, take a vacation from it for a couple
of weeks, try not to think about it, then

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:38.310
reread it again, make more notes and go back
to work on it.

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:50.080
And it’s a way that a lot of neophyte screenwriters
(new screenwriters) don’t do or don’t

00:43:50.080 --> 00:43:59.960
pursue which is you’ve got to be as a writer,
as a craftsperson you must be relentless in

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:07.210
your goal of creating quality material.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:11.480
For instance (I’ll give you a for instance).

00:44:11.480 --> 00:44:17.510
I’m working with a young lady right now
who was one of our grad students a few years

00:44:17.510 --> 00:44:19.200
ago
(2-3 years ago).

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:25.170
She’s been my class assistant and stuff
like that so we knew each other pretty well.

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:33.520
She took her thesis screenplay from (I don’t
know) three years ago and I read it and told

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:37.060
her “You’ve got a great idea here…you
really do.

00:44:37.060 --> 00:44:38.720
I believe in this idea.”

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:43.210
And we had conversations such as there is
no such thing as a bad screenplay, only an

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:45.910
unfinished one.

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:50.400
And I gave her some pointers and scribbled
things on the pages and she went back and

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:52.420
wrote it again (this is a feature film).

00:44:52.420 --> 00:44:56.589
And I looked it and was “You know what?

00:44:56.589 --> 00:44:57.589
This is better.”

00:44:57.589 --> 00:45:02.410
And I went through and the sample editing
and the scribble, scribble kind of stuff.

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:06.990
And she looks at it and it’s a little bit
better.

00:45:06.990 --> 00:45:15.640
She’s been through that process (well in
this case with me) it must be 6 times now.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.390
I mean years have passed.

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:22.109
She had a draft of this when she went up for
her thesis 3 years ago right?

00:45:22.109 --> 00:45:25.760
But I’ll tell you and I’m reading it once
again.

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:32.650
I’ll probably be looking at it again this
weekend, she is so close.

00:45:32.650 --> 00:45:39.040
I kept telling her keep track of…don’t
lose your draft of your first draft, don’t

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:43.040
lose a copy of your first draft because a
day is coming when I would like to have that

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:48.910
in my hand and your final draft and put that
together in a binder and use this in class

00:45:48.910 --> 00:45:53.780
and have people read the first draft and now
read the last draft.

00:45:53.780 --> 00:46:01.140
It could be a wonderful, wonderful exploration
of teaching writing because this has become

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:03.790
viable.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:12.890
She is now close to having an entirely shootable,
casting, really good social commentary screenplay.

00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:19.320
Film Courage: What changed in those 6 drafts
over the 3 years or however many?

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:21.060
Professor Edson: Craft.

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:22.060
Craft.

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:28.030
Her mastery of…what most of them do is they
write what they mean and when it comes to

00:46:28.030 --> 00:46:32.010
dialogue, please just blurt it out and it’s
called on-the-nose-dialogue.

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:37.650
That’s one of the things you teach…stop
it!

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:43.560
People do not talk like that, people talk
around what they mean not that there can’t

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:48.860
be confrontation and stuff like that under
certain emotional circumstances but it was

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:56.380
dialogue and less is more in terms of the
amount of dialogue.

00:46:56.380 --> 00:47:04.170
It was description, it was about the use of
language and vocabulary and description using

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:09.730
irrelevant words - the, and, there…there’s
a whole list of the 9 most utterly useless

00:47:09.730 --> 00:47:10.730
words.

00:47:10.730 --> 00:47:15.050
I’ve got it back there in a sheet I pass
out sometimes.

00:47:15.050 --> 00:47:16.380
All that is is filler.

00:47:16.380 --> 00:47:18.370
All that does is slow the reader down.

00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:29.220
It is developing a style in a way you describe
and offer exposition and description in scene,

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:32.650
scene heading, slug lines and then what we’re
looking at in stuff like that.

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:41.540
Drawing people in, where you put things, building
the plot in an ever better way (you can do

00:47:41.540 --> 00:47:44.680
more here) that is what has been going on
for all those years.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:45.680
14.

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:46.680
Value Of An Idea

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:50.700
Richard Walter, UCLA Screenwriting Chairman:
The most overrated part of the screenplay

00:47:50.700 --> 00:47:51.920
equation is the idea.

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:58.010
Ideas are usually pretty useless and worthless.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:07.110
Forgive me if I’m repeating myself I am
one of those people who believes that BREAKING

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:09.150
BAD is one of the greatest achievements in
the history of civilization.

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:13.550
I think it is really great drama.

00:48:13.550 --> 00:48:16.900
So what is the idea that drives it?

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:28.839
A high school chemistry teacher gets a cancer
diagnosis so he decides to go into dealing

00:48:28.839 --> 00:48:34.770
with an incorrigible former student of his
(a criminal) to support his family.

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:37.560
It’s the stupidest idea I’ve ever heard.

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:42.740
Many, many companies shot that down.

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:45.490
And yet it is this triumphant achievement.

00:48:45.490 --> 00:49:00.770
62 hours of TV series, every frame of which
is engaging and captivating and involving.

00:49:00.770 --> 00:49:01.770
So how did that happen?

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:03.240
And the answer is they told a good story.

00:49:03.240 --> 00:49:11.200
It’s where the story, it’s really all
about story and at UCLA that’s what I believe.

00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:15.050
Imagine if someone came up to you and said
“Hey I have an idea for a movie.

00:49:15.050 --> 00:49:19.490
This guys stutters but he has to give a speech.

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:20.680
They hire a speech therapist.

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:24.260
They work on this speech and he gives this
speech at the end of the movie.”

00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:28.890
If somebody told you that that’s going to
win the Oscar for best picture and best screenplay,

00:49:28.890 --> 00:49:31.060
you’d tell them they are crazy.

00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:34.340
And yet that is of course THE KING’S SPEECH.

00:49:34.340 --> 00:49:43.730
And again I think it just demonstrates the
value of story and the valuelessness of ideas.

00:49:43.730 --> 00:49:48.150
And I think that very young people are more
into ideas, they have great ideas.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:52.320
I like to say if you have a great idea, a
really, really great idea for a screenplay,

00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:54.660
that’s all you’ve got.

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:57.450
I mean what remains after that?

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:58.450
Everything.

00:49:58.450 --> 00:50:03.569
The characters have to be invented, the dialogue
they speak has to be created.

00:50:03.569 --> 00:50:11.360
It has to be punchy and peppy and provocative
and pungent (I’m just getting into P’s

00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:17.310
now) and poetic and it has to be worth listening
to all for itself just because there is something

00:50:17.310 --> 00:50:18.890
kind of charming about it.

00:50:18.890 --> 00:50:20.850
But beyond that it can’t just be all for
itself.

00:50:20.850 --> 00:50:27.200
It also has to advance the story in a palpable,
measurable, identifiable way.

00:50:27.200 --> 00:50:29.890
Likewise expand the audiences appreciation
of the characters.

00:50:29.890 --> 00:50:36.869
It takes time for me to give you an idea about
a movie it takes about a handful of seconds

00:50:36.869 --> 00:50:40.640
to walk you through the story which takes
the length of the movie (a couple of hours).

00:50:40.640 --> 00:50:43.200
So that’s where the value is.

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:44.200
15.

00:50:44.200 --> 00:50:45.200
Story Structure Myth

00:50:45.200 --> 00:50:55.290
Larry Wilson, Screenwriter: There is a huge
business (maybe an overly-huge business) in

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:58.060
teaching screenplay structure.

00:50:58.060 --> 00:51:03.640
A 3-act structure, a 5-act structure.

00:51:03.640 --> 00:51:14.250
And I know a fair share of screenwriting gurus
who are at each others throats of which structure

00:51:14.250 --> 00:51:22.960
is the structure and I’m not saying there
is not a structure.

00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:29.180
I would be in big trouble if I said there
wasn’t a structure and that a story doesn’t

00:51:29.180 --> 00:51:31.990
have a beginning, a middle and an end.

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:49.500
But this reliance, this absolute need to follow
any structure map that’s out there and if

00:51:49.500 --> 00:51:53.560
you don’t have a turning point on page 30
you haven’t done it right.

00:51:53.560 --> 00:51:59.869
Or if you’re on step 5 of 22 steps, you’re
on the wrong step, you haven’t done it right

00:51:59.869 --> 00:52:05.300
or any of this stuff it’s not true.

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:13.829
This structure business, it’s a business
and you need a diagram to teach structure

00:52:13.829 --> 00:52:23.810
and you sell a diagram and it’s very hard
to say I have the best diagram unless you

00:52:23.810 --> 00:52:25.750
have a diagram.

00:52:25.750 --> 00:52:28.950
I mean you need a diagram to sell a diagram.

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:34.710
I told you earlier about my diagram that ends
on page 60 with someone dangling down.

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:36.500
It’s an emotional diagram.

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:42.590
It goes like this…Page 1 I’m going to
do this, this is going to be the best story

00:52:42.590 --> 00:52:46.690
ever, Page 10 Oh my gosh I can really do this.

00:52:46.690 --> 00:52:48.250
Everyone was wrong about me.

00:52:48.250 --> 00:52:52.460
My parents can go F themselves!

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:53.920
I’m a writer.

00:52:53.920 --> 00:53:00.490
On and on emotional and like Page 20 you go
this is really getting hard, the graph starts

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:05.609
going down and down and then you get to Page
60 and it’s like I’ll never finish this.

00:53:05.609 --> 00:53:07.710
It’s my struggle.

00:53:07.710 --> 00:53:09.579
It’s an emotional structure.

00:53:09.579 --> 00:53:12.120
It’s an emotional diagram.

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:16.930
Film Courage: Wait…sorry to interrupt but
what happens after the hanging diagram?

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:21.070
Larry: Oh well you go into despair for awhile.

00:53:21.070 --> 00:53:26.060
You put yourself to bed and then you wake
up and start again and it has a happy ending.

00:53:26.060 --> 00:53:32.980
And at the ending it has a happy ending, big
bags of money, all the love and companionship

00:53:32.980 --> 00:53:37.290
you ever wanted, power, you win right?

00:53:37.290 --> 00:53:42.760
But it’s obviously tongue-in-cheek and to
make a point.

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:52.410
But the real point of it is 
have characters who you believe in and you

00:53:52.410 --> 00:53:57.940
believe that you can write them and they are
in some sense writing you, that they are coming

00:53:57.940 --> 00:54:00.420
out of a place of truth for you.

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:06.890
Put them in the tightest, most impossible
situation you can imagine and get them out

00:54:06.890 --> 00:54:08.910
of it.

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:18.690
And know that you have probably (if you are
writing a screenplay) in a 100 to 120 minutes.

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:27.710
And let the story flow and there will be natural
points within you doing that where you will

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:35.500
feel needs to go faster, needs to go slower,
that there needs to be more drama, there needs

00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:40.000
to be a moment of rest, all these things that
structure teaches you and again I’m not

00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:42.200
saying there isn’t a structure.

00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:51.150
But those structure diagrams they can assist
you or they can be a cage.

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:59.930
They can be an absolute cage and they can
trap you and if you believe that you’re

00:54:59.930 --> 00:55:05.670
a writer, you have a natural storytelling
gift that you need to honor, that you need

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:08.160
to respect or don’t be a writer.

00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:13.960
Don’t think that you can tell a story if
you don’t have a natural storytelling gift

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:18.599
and you can’t tell when things are going
too slowly or things are going to fast and

00:55:18.599 --> 00:55:24.220
just let if flow and then go back and see
if it’s structured right.

00:55:24.220 --> 00:55:30.440
But this is another thing that will stop people
in their tracts over and over again if your

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:33.030
first act is too long.

00:55:33.030 --> 00:55:34.030
Who cares?

00:55:34.030 --> 00:55:35.610
Who cares?

00:55:35.610 --> 00:55:42.250
You’ll figure that out if writing is rewriting.

00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:51.430
And watch a lot of movies, read a lot of books,
I’m now because of constraints of time sometimes

00:55:51.430 --> 00:55:53.569
I’m a podcast junkie.

00:55:53.569 --> 00:55:59.490
I hear the most brilliant stories in podcasts,
stories that have natural, beginning, middle

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:01.940
and an end because they are true.

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:06.520
Just embrace all of that, embrace the movies
that you love, take them to your heart.

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:11.589
And if you’re a storyteller, the story will
emerge and it will emerge with a structure.

00:56:11.589 --> 00:56:13.809
BONUS - Never forget this one.

00:56:13.809 --> 00:56:18.569
Blayne Weaver, Actor, Screenwriter, Director,
Producer: The biggest lesson about writing

00:56:18.569 --> 00:56:20.640
get up and do it.

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:21.980
It is a job.

00:56:21.980 --> 00:56:28.640
I’m the kind of guy that I like a glass
of wine, I like some music going, that’s

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:32.960
very romantic and I’m sure Hemingway would
be very proud but that’s not really how

00:56:32.960 --> 00:56:39.420
you get a script done or how you write that
many scripts and I am always writing so it’s

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:42.400
like you’ve got to get up and spend time
with your coffee.

00:56:42.400 --> 00:56:43.780
You’ve got to look at it.

00:56:43.780 --> 00:56:48.130
Sometimes it’s just sitting there not being
able to write anything that gets you 2 days

00:56:48.130 --> 00:56:52.940
later to where you can answer the question
on paper.

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:58.040
Celeste Chaney, Author: Everybody says you
have to write and read everyday because it’s

00:56:58.040 --> 00:56:59.130
true.

00:56:59.130 --> 00:57:03.839
If you are writing everyday, the next day
you are going to write a little better than

00:57:03.839 --> 00:57:09.020
you did the day before and in 4 years you
are going to be a much better writer than

00:57:09.020 --> 00:57:11.250
you were 4 years ago.

00:57:11.250 --> 00:57:17.060
I think a lot of writers are waiting for the
world to give you permission to pursue your

00:57:17.060 --> 00:57:21.190
craft and you need to give yourself that permission.

00:57:21.190 --> 00:57:25.220
You need to show up and say Okay I’m going
to do this and I’m probably not going to

00:57:25.220 --> 00:57:30.309
be very good right now or maybe I am great
and I don’t know it yet.

00:57:30.309 --> 00:57:33.240
You won’t know either way unless you put
yourself out there.

00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:39.160
And sitting down everyday whether that’s
for an hour in the morning, an hour in the

00:57:39.160 --> 00:57:44.160
evening, somewhere in-between the timing doesn’t
matter and it really doesn’t even matter

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:47.730
if you’re writing a 1,000 words or if you’re
writing 50.

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:49.410
The important thing is that you’re writing.

